\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03557524-3809-52d4-9d68-9faf937bdb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564600} {:answer/id #uuid "224f63a5-963c-5451-b6e8-7fba150db1a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564602} {:answer/id #uuid "343fd6ee-2f12-525f-b827-6036ba3e52e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564609} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8cec09-fbba-518e-ad05-89fcd612bc74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564626} {:answer/id #uuid "d98c2129-2808-5ee4-8422-3f6a6eeb389d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03557524-3809-52d4-9d68-9faf937bdb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564600} :answer-field/id #uuid "d22be944-ac35-5b9d-84ab-bd2397fe1885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09e5259b-47e9-5e22-b6a8-c7e9106ef293" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${120}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29381743-bc0a-58b7-b7c6-16932dd9f9e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29381743-bc0a-58b7-b7c6-16932dd9f9e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564607} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f7196b-f145-51aa-93ef-8dcbc705481c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564616} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248e1098-29d9-543b-83d4-0b32e84165eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564605}] :answer-field/corre