t $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a03dda-1f79-54bd-8fde-67abfc008cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63a03dda-1f79-54bd-8fde-67abfc008cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564614} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c4c7d41-9d91-55af-a829-2d44cb358552" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564621} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fca26e85-1a1e-5672-a486-f4d949c36d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fca26e85-1a1e-5672-a486-f4d949c36d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564653} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e994e7b-c5b3-51a5-bc01-6069ba4346c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a830f9c3-0121-5788-b38e-170711f9179f" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2\\sqrt{3},$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${240}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=2\\sqrt{3},\\,\\theta ={240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2442c3e4-6efc-5e15-89f6-7d2d57190032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{15}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564604} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1385f2-403d-5b5e-96cd-323ad27d5421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{15}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564608} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef219a0-8020-5eec-87fe-43e0abef3ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{15}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564610} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca1a3c5-4637-51a8-82a3-9937d8eddb83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{15}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564618} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6134d2-8694-5624-a3fc-ee67d2bcafca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ef219a0-8020-5eec-87fe-43e0abef3ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{15}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564610} :answer-field/id #uuid "6579e5a5-9106-52c9-8fc7-288e42ec2e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ab66f3c8-a163-5ed5-96e6-683323bcc0cb" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $5\\sqrt{3}$, and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{2π}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=5\\sqrt{3},\\,\\theta =\\frac{2π}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2π}{3}) \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2π}{3}) \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{15}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83edfaff-4222-5e9e-a519-228b47973acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83edfaff-4222-5e9e-a519-228b47973acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564622} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6834b98-d3c2-5dd4-be0b-fef3999f298b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564645} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b4b65ac-05e7-5888-8be4-c434115530bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b4b65ac-05e7-5888-8be4-c434115530bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564613} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb46acc9-7536-549f-8767-afd199ad67aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b868a554-dcd1-5408-a2ec-e25b8176933b" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${240}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3553956-3877-5961-8909-451bd82660b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3553956-3877-5961-8909-451bd82660b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564635} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ad2c603-5053-52ae-bd55-05d1ae8a2fe8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564625} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d61935b3-8390-52b2-a64e-02c865765d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d61935b3-8390-52b2-a64e-02c865765d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564644} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea0bc07c-4872-5236-a36a-31e19fca1510" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "efc6076b-cc8c-5b74-b97a-015436679a5e" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $4\\sqrt{2},$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=4\\sqrt{2},\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbbf92a9-0fa4-55a1-bb95-e955f0f6000e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564640}] :answer-f