\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(5,5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d89868-a75f-579e-a975-aee85bb121db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50d89868-a75f-579e-a975-aee85bb121db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564612} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6850feb-e59a-57fa-8eb3-b345830389df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564630} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fae41de2-30dc-52c2-b767-4cd2fca0ebfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae41de2-30dc-52c2-b767-4cd2fca0ebfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564651} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb4bb037-4458-5443-9885-b84764763734" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f379e31b-1455-531c-aca1-32e394f270e2" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${45}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}]}
